    "https://www.brandonu.ca/jobs/job/bailey-the-bobcat-mascot-2/": {
      "title": "BrandonUniversity",
      "description": "Bailey The Bobcat Mascot | Employment Opportunities Top Toolbar Links Webmail Campus Map Contact Us Brandon University Search Site: Primary Navigation Future Students Programs Current Students Athletics Alumni Services Brandon University Employment Opportunities Bailey The Bobcat Mascot Bailey The Bobcat Mascot Applications are to be received by: 4:30 p.m. on September 7, 2026. Classification: Student Assistant II Job Title: Bailey the Bobcat Mascot Department: Athletics Wage or Salary: $17.63/hr Hours of Work: varies per week; 6 – 10 hours per week Start Date: September 11, 2026 Number of Positions available: 2 Length of Appointment: 09/11/2026 to 08/31/2027 Date Issued: August 3 1, 2026 Duties: 1. Be energetic, enthusiastic and be able to engage with the crowd at Bobcat games and special events 2. Work well with children 3. Be available to work predominately evenings and weekends at games. There may be some special events that require daytime attendance 4. Be comfortable participating in and/or lead half-time and between set entertainment during Bobcat games 5. Attend community functions and special events as Bailey 6. Other related duties as assigned. Qualifications/Experience: 1. Previous experience working with children is an asset, but not required 2. Ability to work independently with minimal supervision and/or with others effectively 3. Physical literacy – the successful candidate can move, dance, etc within the mascot costume 4. Interest and knowledge of sports (specifically basketball, volleyball and soccer) is an asset but not required. **Must provide Criminal Record Check and Child Abuse Registry Check upon hire To apply please send a resume to: Russ Paddock, Director of Athletics & Recreation paddockr@brandonu.ca Brandon University Bobcats Brandon University is committed to equity, welcomes diversity, and hires on the basis of merit. All qualified individuals who may contribute to the diversification of the University, especially w